              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_the_jones_polynomial"} Relation to the Jones polynomial:
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_homology_of_loop_spaces_of_configuration_spaces"} Relation to homology of loop spaces of configuration spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#as_chernsimons_amplitudes"} As Chern-Simons amplitudes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#as_observables_on_fuzzy_spheres"} As observables on fuzzy spheres
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#vassiliev_invariants_of_braids"} Vassiliev invariants of braids
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"} knots
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            (or in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+loop+space"} smooth loop space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} Knot invariants
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+functions"} locally constant functions
            on this manifold. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of the space of knots is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discriminant"} discriminant
            and consists of all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+knots"} singular knots
          %p
            If we consider those singular knots with only a finite number of double points, we can build a
            %span.newWikiWord
              cubical complex
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cubical+complex"} ?
            from this data. The vertices in the complex are labelled by the isotopy classes of knots, and more generally the
            = succeed "-cubes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            double points (and a few other technical pieces of information). The boundary operator resolves a double crossing either upwards or downwards according to the orientation at the crossing.
          %p
            A
            %strong Vassiliev invariant
            is simply a cubical morphism from this complex to an abelian group that vanishes above a certain degree.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            One does not need the language of cubical complexes to
            %em define
            Vassiliev invariants. Rather, there is a general method whereby a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
            can be extended to all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+knots"} singular knots
            with only finitely many double points (and no other singularities) using the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vassiliev+skein+relations"} Vassiliev skein relations
          #vinv.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Vassiliev invariant
              of degree (or order)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le n
              is a knot invariant whose extension to singular knots (with double points) vanishes on all singular knots with more than
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              double points.
          %p
            As is standard, it is of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            if it is of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le n
            but not
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le n - 1
            = succeed "." do
              %strong finite type invariants
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The degree of Vassiliev invariants defines a filtration on the space of knots (and more particularly, on the
              = succeed ")." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  algebra of knots
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/algebra+of+knots"} ?
              = succeed "-equivalent" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le n
              agree on them. In particular, a knot that is
              = succeed "-equivalent" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-trivial." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_chord_diagrams_and_weight_systems Relation to Chord diagrams and weight systems
          %p
            A function which is constant on nonsingular knots may be extended to a Vassiliev invariant of degree 0 by applying the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vassiliev+skein+relations"} Vassiliev skein relations
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+knot"} singular knot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : S^1 \to \mathbb{R}^3
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            distinct double points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1,\dots,x_n \in \mathbb{R}^3
            gives rise to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chord+diagram"} chord diagram
            of order
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            with a chord connecting each pair of points
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(x_1), \dots, f^{-1}(x_n)
          %p The importance of this construction for singular knots comes from the fact that any finite type invariant determines a function on chord diagrams:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              be a Vassiliev invariant of degree
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              on a singular knot with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              distinct double points depends only on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chord+diagram"} chord diagram
              of the singular knot, and not on the knot itself.
          %p
            Conversely, one can ask which functions on chord diagrams come from finite type invariants. The answer is that Vassiliev invariants (of degree
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le n
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems"} weight systems
            (of order
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1T+relation"} 1T relation
            = succeed "\u201D:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4T+relation"} 4T relation
            %a{:href => "#BarNatan95"} Bar-Natan 95
            (or Theorem 6.2.13 of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#LandoZvonkin"} Lando &amp; Zvonkin
          #WeightSystemsAreAssociatedGradedOfVassilievInvariants.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems+are+associated+graded+of+Vassiliev+invariants"}> weight systems are associated graded of Vassiliev invariants
                )
            %p
